{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788350813,"nonce":"mZVrXRk70SRLzknMg5LCIqJoS1c=","title":"**Depends on `eef837a`, and overlaps it deliberately.** `eef837a` acceptance item 4 states this same requirement from the butcher bill's side; this issue states it as the general bill-health rule so it is not lost if `eef837a` ships narrowly. One worker should close both. Filed separately for the record rather than folded, so the round's ledger matches the compiled list in `RUNS/m1-20260901/next-run.md`.","message":"`Bill.next_ingredient_search_tick` in the **future** means the bill ran a failed ingredient search and backed off. It is a sleep state, and it outlives its cause: a bill that failed while its ingredients were forbidden stays dead long after they are unforbidden. Re-creating the bill resets the field to 0, which is why \"delete and re-add the bill\" reads as a fix.\n\n`production-still-runs` already names the field as *\"the only observable proof either way\"*. It is published, and nothing flags a future value. In run `m1-20260901` it sat in the future for days on the butcher bill while the colony starved, and no checklist line looks at it.\n\n\n1. `bills` reports the sleep state as a state, not as a raw tick a caller must compare against `now`: a boolean or a named status plus the tick it wakes, so a reader who does not know the convention still sees it.\n2. A bill that has failed N consecutive ingredient searches is reported as such, distinct from a bill that is suspended and from a bill that is working.\n3. The distinction between \"asleep and will retry\" and \"asleep and will retry against a filter that can never match\" is drawn where the mod can draw it — the second is `eef837a`'s defect and should not present identically.\n4. `checklists/turn.md` cites the field at the production read.\n5. Covered by `eef837a`'s acceptance script rather than a second one, if the same worker ships both.","files":null}]}